#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# map

#% text
# `map(f, xs)` gives the list `[f(x1), f(x2), ...]` of `f` applied to each element of the list `xs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `f` may be a function name, a lambda `x => ...`, a slot function `&(...)` or any expression that can be called.

#% item
# The results are collected in order; `map(f, [])` is `[]`.

#% item
# Only the top level of `xs` is visited: a nested list is passed to `f` whole.

#% item
# Many arithmetic functions are listable and act on lists by themselves, so `sin(xs)` needs no `map`.

#% item
# In a pipe, `xs |> map(f, _)` puts the list in the second argument.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
map(f, [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [f(a), f(b), f(c)]

#% cell
map(x => x^2, [1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= [1, 4, 9, 16]

#% cell
map(length, [[1], [1, 2], []])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 0]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A slot function:

#% cell
map(&(&1 + 10), [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [11, 12, 13]

#% text
# Each element of a list of pairs is one argument:

#% cell
map(p => p[1]*p[2], [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]])
#% output
#= [2, 12, 30]

#% text
# Any callable value, here a function built by another function:

#% cell
map(derivative(1)(sin), [0, pi/2, pi])
#% output
#= [1, 0, -1]

#% text
# In a pipe:

#% cell
range(5) |> map(k => factorial(k), _)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 6, 24, 120]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Evaluating a function at sample points:

#% cell
map(x => n(exp(-x^2)), range(0, 2, 1/2))
#% output
#= [1.0, 0.7788007830714049, 0.36787944117144233, 0.10539922456186433, 0.01831563888873418]

#% text
# The lengths of the words of a sentence:

#% cell
map(string_length, string_split("maps apply functions elementwise"))
#% output
#= [4, 5, 9, 11]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `map` keeps the length of the list:

#% cell
length(map(x => 0, range(7)))
#% output
#= 7

#% text
# For listable functions, applying to the list directly gives the same:

#% cell
map(sqrt, [1, 4, 9]) === sqrt([1, 4, 9])
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `map_indexed` also passes the position, `map_thread` walks several lists together:

#% cell
map_indexed((x, i) => x^i, [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [a, b^2, c^3]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A nested list is passed whole; map twice to reach the inner elements:

#% cell
map(x => x + 1, [[1, 2], [3]])
#% output
#= [[2, 3], [4]]

#% cell
map(row => map(x => x^2, row), [[1, 2], [3]])
#% output
#= [[1, 4], [9]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `map_indexed`, `map_thread`, `select`, `table`, `fold`, `apply`
